    "profile": {
        "business": "telecommunications and media conglomerate (Xfinity cable/internet, NBCUniversal, Sky); the Michigan facility functions as network and business-services infrastructure supporting Comcast's own cable, internet, and Comcast Business operations rather than third-party wholesale colocation",
        "ownership": "public, NASDAQ ticker CMCSA, SEC CIK 0001166691",
        "research_note": "Exact Michigan facility address, capacity, and function not confirmed from operator-published sources as of research date; recorded as a Comcast network/business facility based on third-party data center directory listings.",
        "research_status": "complete"
    },
    "reading": "Comcast Corporation is a cable, broadband and media company headquartered in Philadelphia, listed on NASDAQ as CMCSA and filing with the Securities and Exchange Commission under central index key 0001166691. It is in this registry as an enterprise operator, not a colocation provider. Cable companies appear in data centre inventories because the physical architecture of a cable network requires them to run buildings full of computing and transmission equipment, and those buildings look like data centres from the outside and increasingly like data centres on the inside.\n\nComcast's own annual report describes the shape of that estate without naming any part of it. Its business section refers to headends, fibre optic and coaxial cable owned or leased by the company, equipment such as lasers, routers, switches and content distribution servers, national and regional data centres, network operations centres, and equipment placed at locations held under agreements with local public utilities and municipalities. A regional technical facility of this kind aggregates video and broadband traffic, terminates transport, hosts content delivery caching so that streaming traffic does not have to traverse the backbone, and carries the systems behind Comcast Business services for commercial customers. None of it is sold as colocation space.\n\nThe building recorded here is at 14312 Northline Road in Southgate, Michigan, a single structure in the Detroit Downriver suburbs whose footprint record gives a construction year of 1998. The source inventory also carries a second Comcast building at 7059 South Potomac Street in Centennial, Colorado, tagged as an Xfinity and Comcast data centre, although this operator row records Michigan only.\n\nThe local record is thin in a specific and checkable way. The City of Southgate's master plan treats Northline Road as an urban other principal arterial and gives a traffic count of about 37,661 average daily trips between Allen Road and Interstate 75, but it names no telecommunications or data facility along the corridor and does not list the city's largest employers. There is no rezoning, conditional use or siting proceeding attached to the building in the material this registry could find, which is consistent with a facility that predates the current wave of data centre permitting fights.\n\nWhat is not established is almost everything a capacity researcher would want. Comcast publishes no floor area, no utility service figure, no generator or UPS rating and no function statement for the Southgate building, and its annual report names no individual technical facility. The company does publish state level investment figures for Michigan, more than $2.3 billion over three years covering wages, benefits, taxes and charitable giving with more than $855 million of that in network capital spending, but it does not break any of that out by site.",
